      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Oh no please don't.\n\nIf you don't like that the world changes your avatar size, don't visit it. That is where your personal freedom should end. Maybe with an exception to private instances, but not in a public one.\n\nWorlds have suffered long enough from people using small avatars or giant avatars where it is not appropriate and disrupts the experience of others. Especially competitive game worlds must be able to override your personal height to make it fair for everyone else, but other worlds may also decide that you cannot be larger than the room you are in, because it disrupts the vision of everyone else if you put your 200m avatars (with a foot larger than the entire world) in it. The current system is already very limited, because you cannot prevent people from using the uploaded avatar size by setting allowed limits alone. This should be enough freedom. But to allow people to turn any avatar into whatever size they want is an absolute nightmare in some worlds. If people go as far as detecting and overriding your avatar scale in Udon, they probably have a very good reason to do so.\n\nNot to mention how this would break the experience for people who have this disabled if a feature in the world depends on scaling the user, e.g. to fit into a small vehicle. People would complain that something doesn't work for their avatar size and put the blame on the world (trust me, happens every time).\n\nIf you don't like what the world is doing, don't go there. It's really that simple. Leave a little bit of decision-making for us world creators and vote against it by not being in our world if you can't handle the tiny bit of decision-making that we are allowed to do regarding avatars. \n\nI'd love to limit people from bringing their 10 trillion polygon avatars into a public lobby as well, but I guess that would also hurt someone's freedom to crash other users?\n\nIf you want to lobby for more freedom in private instances only, to which I could agree in most scenarios, we would only need an Udon function that allows us to know the instance type to allow more freedom in them. That would make way more sense for everyone. It should however still be the world's decision to decide what sizes they allow in them. \n\n99,9% of worlds won't bother to restrict anything, and those who do have a good reason for it.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
    {
