      "value": "@{5f3287055bc2dc276e0f1630|full_name}: The world author should have *NO* control or say on what users do in private instances. It's private; no one cares. Everyone in there knows each other and any scale-related problems can be solved in-house with words instead of UDON bruteforcing.\n\nPublic? Oh absolutely. Strangers can't be trusted. Game worlds especially; they need to make things fair.\n\nBut if I want to be 12ft tall and have my friends rest on me while we watch movies in an Invite+ instance, I should be able to do that.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2023-06-23T21:43:36.907Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "With the recent beta allowing for scaling in worlds my major concern is the balance of agency between users and world creators.  As it is currently implemented world creators have a monopoly on power, they can restrict the ability of users to change their own scale, (which is perfectly reasonable) and they have the ability to change user scale using Udon which is fantastic for games and adventure worlds.\n\nMy concern stems from this second ability which removed player agency over the size of their avatar.  Many people I know have uploaded their avatars at a specific scale whether to be small and cute or because they want their avatar to match their actual height in real life.  Allowing world creators the ability to completely override any user agency over the size of their avatar, their personification in VRChat, is unacceptable because it removes a component of individuality from a user’s creation.\n\nMy proposal for this discrepancy in agency is to allow a user to opt out of scaling.  For example, a world creator has disabled user controlled scaling and automatically scales your avatar to say 5 feet.  The user would be able to go into their menu and disable any avatar scaling thus preserving their upload scale and artistic intent of their avatar.  This brings agency over their scale back to the user without removing the agency of world creators to prevent people from changing their scale to exploit or be obnoxious.",
